* Fri Oct 23 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.1.2-2
- No code change. BuildRequires libstdc++-static, which the source build has
  always needed: it links libstdc++ statically on purpose, and Fedora ships
  libstdc++.a only in that package. CI's first Fedora build of the source arm
  stopped at the link with "cannot find -lstdc++". The Debian dev box, where
  the --prebuilt arm is built, never saw it, because Debian ships libstdc++.a
  with the compiler. Reissued as a new release because the source package
  changed, and two different artifacts must not share one NVR.

* Fri Oct 16 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.1.2-1
- Stop probing an empty binder after the session ends, which is the other half
  of the leak 1.1.1 fixed at startup. 1.1.1 gated the first
  gbinder_servicemanager_wait() on GetSession and left the gate one-shot, so
  the daemon still paid the full cost at the other end of a session: when the
  container goes, libgbinder arms its own presence-check timer, which probes
  handle 0 on a delay that backs off from 100 ms to a 1 s ceiling and then
  stays there, and every probe that lands on an unowned handle costs two kernel
  log lines. Measured on bigtab01 on a boot that had reached the greeter with
  zero binder lines to its name: one logout produced 360 of them in the next
  3 minutes, 2 a second and still climbing when the sample ended, and 379 of
  that boot's 428 binder lines were the daemon's own pid.
- The daemon now notices and stops. A dead service manager arms a session
  watch, which asks GetSession every 2 s, and a container manager that answers
  with no sessions ends the pass: the service manager reference is dropped --
  which is what actually stops the probing, since libgbinder's finalize()
  removes that timer and its dispose() drops the device from the per-device
  instance cache -- and the daemon returns to the 1.1.1 session gate to wait
  for the next container, touching no binder while it waits.
- A loop, NOT an exit, and that distinction is the design. Exiting and letting
  Restart=always bring the daemon back would have been less code and would have
  reversed the property the unit is built around -- the daemon outlives the
  container, which is why it is deliberately not ordered after
  waydroid-container.service -- and it would have broken docs/34's
  start-it-by-hand mode outright, where nothing exists to restart it and the
  first logout would have been the last.
- Does not drop a registration Android is coming back for. A system_server
  restart under a live session and a logout are indistinguishable at the moment
  of death, and acting on the wrong one is docs/16's stale binder that
  deadlocked Android's network stack for 38 minutes. GetSession separates them,
  but not immediately: container_manager.stop() clears args.session AFTER
  taking the container down, measured at about 6 s on this host, which is why
  the watch polls instead of asking once. A service manager that reappears
  cancels the watch and re-registers in place, exactly as before.
- Fails towards staying up, in every direction: no system bus, a GetSession
  that errors, or one that never answers at all leaves the daemon where it is.
  After 60 s of no answer it stops asking and leaves the probe running, which
  is the pre-1.1.2 behaviour and keeps a waydroid that does not offer
  GetSession working.
- No new runtime dependency, checked rather than assumed: libgio-2.0 and
  libgobject-2.0 were already DT_NEEDED entries, because the 1.1.1 startup gate
  already called g_bus_get_sync().
- Fixes an exit status that was wrong on a path nothing had exercised: with one
  pass per process, app.ret could be left RET_OK by a session that registered
  successfully, so a later session whose wificond registration failed -- the
  one failure this daemon treats as fatal -- would have exited 0. It is now set
  per pass.

* Fri Oct 16 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.1.1-2
- No code change: the daemon's object code is identical to 1.1.1-1. This
  rebuild corrects a comment in wifi/service.cpp that claimed the gate opens
  "before the container's init runs". It does not -- container_manager.do_start
  assigns args.session AFTER helpers.lxc.start() -- and the real margins were
  measured rather than argued once that was noticed. Reissued rather than
  rebuilt in place because 1.1.1-1 had already been installed, and two
  different artifacts must not share one NVR.
- Ships docs/69-wifid-binder-ping-spam.md, which is where the measurements and
  the two open questions now live.

* Fri Oct 16 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.1.1-1
- Stop pinging an empty binder while no session exists. The daemon entered
  gbinder_servicemanager_wait(sm, -1) at startup, which polls handle 0 at 1 Hz,
  and every probe that lands while nothing owns handle 0 costs two kernel log
  lines. On a tablet that boots to a greeter and waits, that is unbounded: one
  53-hour logged-out stretch on bigtab01 produced 190,501 of them from this one
  call -- 99.94% of that boot's binder lines -- and across all boots the spam
  reached 29.3% of the journal, roughly 300 MB of 1 GB (docs/69).
- Not only noise. binder's own rate limiter began dropping messages, 19
  "callbacks suppressed" in a single boot, which discards unrelated binder
  diagnostics on a host where binder evidence has been load-bearing (docs/16,
  docs/40).
- The probe is now gated on id.waydro.Container's GetSession, which answers an
  empty a{ss} until a session exists and costs no binder traffic. Measured: 30 s
  with no session adds 0 kernel lines where it previously added 60, and the
  daemon never opens the binder device at all.
- Registration is NOT delayed, which is the one thing this could not afford to
  get wrong -- Android caches a failure to reach wifinl80211 (the 38-minute
  deadlock in docs/16). Measured rather than argued: do_start assigns
  args.session after helpers.lxc.start(), so the gate opens once the container
  has been started, which timed at ~1.4 s before servicemanager appears and
  6.7 s before Android's Wi-Fi framework makes its first call. A restart
  against a live session registers both names 4 ms after opening the device.
- Falls back to the old unguarded probe if GetSession never answers within 60 s,
  so a waydroid that does not offer the method still gets a working daemon.

* Thu Oct 01 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.1.0-1
- Read the radio directly over nl80211 for the four things NetworkManager
  cannot answer, via a new Nl80211.cpp that talks raw AF_NETLINK and adds no
  runtime dependency at all -- libnl is not linked and the binary still needs
  exactly the six libraries 1.0.1 did (docs/60).
- Report the NEGOTIATED PHY in getConnectionCapabilities, so Android's "Wi-Fi
  standard" stops reading UNKNOWN. The PHY comes from which MCS attribute the
  driver sent on NL80211_STA_INFO_TX_BITRATE, which is the negotiated one by
  construction; NM's only candidate was the AP's advertised MaxBitrate, which
  measured 1170 Mb/s on a 20 MHz 2.4 GHz channel and so cannot be inverted into
  a PHY. The enum mapping was disassembled from this image's own
  service-wifi.jar, not assumed.
- Answer getDeviceWiphyCapabilities instead of returning null, and this is what
  makes the PHY work reach anything: ThroughputPredictor.predictThroughput logs
  "Null device capabilities passed to throughput predictor" and returns 0 as its
  FIRST act, before looking at the PHY, the width or the streams. Observed
  firing under 1.0.1 and absent since. Max Supported Tx/Rx Link speed go from
  -1 to real values, which the framework can only get from here.
- Report the RX bitrate separately from TX. NM exposes one Bitrate property, so
  rx had always been reported as tx -- 130 Mb/s for both where the live link was
  39 tx and 72.2 rx.
- Take RSSI from NL80211_STA_INFO_SIGNAL rather than inverting NM's 0..100
  quality. The 1.0.1 inverse is exact and stays as the fallback, but NM polls
  the associated AP on its own schedule and measured 3 dB low across five
  consecutive samples; Android now agrees with the driver's dBm on 4 of 4.
- Report real spatial-stream counts instead of a hardcoded 1x1, which had
  understated this 2x2 card by half.
- Report real packet counters in getPacketCounters. Correct but unexercised:
  Android only asks around a link probe, and SendMgmtFrame has never been
  called in this daemon's entire journal on this host. The tx_good/tx_bad
  columns in dumpsys come from getLinkLayerStats, which needs a vendor HAL this
  daemon does not serve, so those stay zero.
- Close the data race docs/59 left open as a design question. mIfname, mDevPath
  and mSelectorMac are now guarded by a recursive mutex, and the nl80211 path is
  keyed by an atomic ifindex so it shares no mutable state at all. A mutex and
  not a post-to-the-main-loop handoff, because the main loop itself calls into
  Android and waiting on it from a binder thread is a cycle with no timeout.

* Wed Sep 30 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.0.1-1
- Fix the RSSI Android is told. NmBackend converted NetworkManager's 0..100
  Strength with quality/2 - 100, which assumes NM's range is -100..-50 dBm; it
  is -100..-40, so the reported value was low by quality/10 dB -- 0 dB at the
  bottom of the scale and 10 dB at the top. The exact inverse is
  -100 + 3*quality/5, verified against nl80211 on 13 same-scan observations and
  across all 61 dBm values NM can represent (docs/59).
- Not cosmetic: this image's ScoringParams are rssi2=-83:-80:-73:-60, so a link
  1 dB shy of "good" was being reported 1.5 dB above "insufficient", and the
  framework's own score history fades that far every few seconds.
- Report channelBandwidth in getConnectionCapabilities, from NM's
  AccessPoint.Bandwidth. technology stays UNKNOWN deliberately: NM exposes only
  the AP's advertised capability, not the negotiated PHY. The parcel layout is
  unchanged, which matters -- this is the handler whose missing non-null marker
  killed system_server on every association in Stage 4.
- Cache the channel width from the host link event rather than calling the
  backend from a binder thread, which would race NmBackend::wifiDevicePath()'s
  writes to mDevPath and mIfname against the GLib main loop.

